        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p><strong>BGP (Border Gateway Protocol)</strong> is the protocol that makes the Internet work. It is designed to exchange routing and reachability information between different Autonomous Systems (AS).</p>
            
            <h3>Autonomous Systems and eBGP/iBGP</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Autonomous System (AS):</strong> A network or group of networks under a single administrative domain, identified by a unique ASN (Autonomous System Number).</li>
                <li><strong>eBGP (External BGP):</strong> Used to exchange routing information <em>between</em> different Autonomous Systems.</li>
                <li><strong>iBGP (Internal BGP):</strong> Used to exchange BGP information <em>within</em> the same Autonomous System, often to transit internet routes across a provider's backbone without redistributing them into the interior gateway protocol.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>BGP Path Attributes</h3>
            <p>BGP does not simply use "cost" or "shortest path routing" (like <a href="ospf-basics.html">OSPF</a>). Instead, it uses a complex set of <strong>path attributes</strong> to determine the best route.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>AS_PATH:</strong> The sequence of ASNs a route has traversed. While BGP prefers a shorter AS_PATH, it is only <em>one</em> part of the best-path decision process.</li>
                <li><strong>NEXT_HOP:</strong> The IP address of the next router to reach the destination. In BGP, this is often the IP of the eBGP peer, not necessarily the immediate connected router.</li>
                <li><strong>LOCAL_PREF:</strong> A conceptual attribute used within an AS to tell all internal routers which outbound path is preferred for a given prefix. Higher LOCAL_PREF wins.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Protocol</th>
                            <th>Scope</th>
                            <th>Metric Type</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>OSPF</td>
                            <td>Interior (IGP)</td>
                            <td>Link Cost (Bandwidth)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>BGP</td>
                            <td>Exterior (EGP)</td>
                            <td>Path Attributes / Policy</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            
            <h3>BGP Use Cases</h3>
            <p>BGP is not for small, single-connection home networks. It is commonly used for Internet routing, multi-homed organizations (connecting to multiple ISPs for redundancy), service providers, and large-scale routing policy enforcement. BGP does NOT automatically choose the lowest-latency route; it chooses paths based on policy.</p>
        
